WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.698 --> 00:00:06.280
Today, ordering sand or rock means picking up the phone, during yard hours.

2
00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:11.638
After 4:30, or on a Sunday, that customer goes looking for someone else.

3
00:00:11.638 --> 00:00:14.615
This is a different way: Order ABC 24/7.

4
00:00:14.615 --> 00:00:20.372
A preview prepared for ABC Sand &amp; Rock by JayCo, AI Consulting &amp; Services.

5
00:00:21.465 --> 00:00:24.038
Step one. The page opens with the whole story:

6
00:00:24.038 --> 00:00:28.651
the customer reaches you after hours, and the order is waiting in the morning.

7
00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:33.298
Three cards show the timeline: a request at 7:40 PM,

8
00:00:33.298 --> 00:00:39.139
tons worked out overnight with ABC's own numbers, and one dispatch board at 5 AM.

9
00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:44.648
A live indicator shows whether the yard is open or closed.

10
00:00:44.648 --> 00:00:47.689
Right now it's closed, and requests still come in.

11
00:00:49.198 --> 00:00:52.045
Step two: the customer, on their phone.

12
00:00:52.045 --> 00:00:55.914
They search 96 real ABC materials, or tap a category.

13
00:00:55.914 --> 00:00:57.843
Let's pick Desert Gold.

14
00:00:59.153 --> 00:01:02.111
Twenty feet by thirty feet, two inches deep.

15
00:01:03.269 --> 00:01:06.618
The page shows its work: 600 square feet,

16
00:01:06.618 --> 00:01:11.357
3.70 cubic yards, times ABC's published 1.4 tons per yard.

17
00:01:11.357 --> 00:01:15.038
That's 5.18 tons, which fits on one truck.

18
00:01:16.579 --> 00:01:19.748
Under one ton, it applies ABC's one-ton minimum.

19
00:01:19.748 --> 00:01:24.157
Over 24 tons, it flags that the order needs more than one load.

20
00:01:26.195 --> 00:01:31.424
Step three: delivery. Sundays are blocked, and on Saturdays the afternoon window is too.

21
00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:33.516
Let's choose a weekday morning.

22
00:01:34.633 --> 00:01:37.616
A few demo fields, and nothing is sent anywhere.

23
00:01:39.495 --> 00:01:44.544
The confirmation shows the reference, the tons, and the window.

24
00:01:44.544 --> 00:01:49.112
The price always reads: call for pricing, (602) 272-1792.

25
00:01:49.112 --> 00:01:51.927
The page never makes up a price.

26
00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:56.976
One tap switches everything to Spanish: "Solicitud recibida."

27
00:01:58.910 --> 00:02:03.375
Step four: the dispatch board that ABC's office would open at 5 AM.

28
00:02:03.375 --> 00:02:06.623
The new request sits at the top, highlighted.

29
00:02:07.959 --> 00:02:10.856
Every other row is clearly labeled example data.

30
00:02:11.995 --> 00:02:16.975
Dispatch can filter by window or status, and sort by date or by tons.

31
00:02:18.604 --> 00:02:20.872
Tons are totaled for each window,

32
00:02:20.872 --> 00:02:25.521
and any order over 24 tons is flagged: needs two or more trucks.

33
00:02:27.268 --> 00:02:32.543
And as the office works, each request moves from New, to Quoted, to Scheduled.

34
00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:36.208
Step five: the missed-call demo.

35
00:02:36.208 --> 00:02:39.814
It's 7:40 PM, the yard is closed, and a customer calls.

36
00:02:39.814 --> 00:02:43.539
This demonstrates what a live AI phone line would do.

37
00:02:44.474 --> 00:02:49.387
The assistant takes the order, works out the tons, offers only real delivery windows,

38
00:02:49.387 --> 00:02:52.874
and says plainly that it's a request, not a price quote.

39
00:02:58.912 --> 00:03:02.630
The same call works in Spanish, for the customer who would rather speak it.

40
00:03:08.555 --> 00:03:12.984
Both requests drop onto the dispatch board, tagged as after-hours calls.

41
00:03:15.677 --> 00:03:17.905
The customer reaches you after hours.

42
00:03:17.905 --> 00:03:20.073
The order is waiting in the morning.

43
00:03:20.073 --> 00:03:25.131
Everything here is a preview built from ABC's public information, with example data.

44
00:03:25.131 --> 00:03:27.790
This is JayCo, AI Consulting &amp; Services.

